  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =-7 \\\\ 2y & =x-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274786/assets/dba24fd68a4972c260c6475c6d037f3fd9e5c24db82e56b33a7f54e24b736469.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{2}$ and passes through the point $(-1,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x+1) \\\\ y & =-2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y=-2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =-2x-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+2x & =\\frac{7}{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=-3.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-1))}^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,1)$ and the line $2y-x=-7$ is $2\\sqrt{5}.$",
